## 4. Variables & Data Types

A variable is a **named container in memory** that holds a value. Every variable must have a declared type.

### Declaration & Initialization

```c
int    age    = 20;          // integer
float  gpa    = 3.9;         // single-precision decimal
double pi     = 3.14159265;  // double-precision decimal
char   grade  = 'A';         // single character (use single quotes)
bool   active = true;        // boolean (needs #include <stdbool.h>)
string name   = "Alice";     // CS50 string (needs #include <cs50.h>)
```

### Data Types References

| Type | Size | Range | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| `int` | 4 bytes | −2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 | `int x = 5;` |
| `long` | 8 bytes | Much larger integers | `long n = 1000000;` |
| `float` | 4 bytes | ~7 decimal digits precision | `float f = 3.14;` |
| `double` | 8 bytes | ~15 decimal digits precision | `double d = 3.14159;` |
| `char` | 1 byte | Single character (0–255) | `char c = 'A';` |
| `bool` | 1 byte | `true` or `false` | `bool b = true;` |

> ⚠️ **Naming Rules:** Variable names can contain letters, digits, `_` but cannot start with a digit. Use descriptive names: `score` not `s`.

