## 5. printf & Format Specifiers

`printf` uses **format specifiers** as placeholders replaced by variable values at runtime.

```c
int age = 20;
printf("I am %i years old.\n", age);
// Output: I am 20 years old.
```

### Format Specifiers

| Specifier | Type | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `%i` or `%d` | `int` | `printf("%i", 42);` → `42` |
| `%f` | `float` / `double` | `printf("%.2f", 3.14159);` → `3.14` |
| `%c` | `char` | `printf("%c", 'A');` → `A` |
| `%s` | `string` | `printf("%s", "Hi");` → `Hi` |
| `%li` | `long` | `printf("%li", 123456789L);` |
| `%%` | Literal `%` | `printf("100%%");` → `100%` |

> **Tip:** Control decimal places with `%.Nf` — e.g., `%.2f` shows 2 decimal places.

### Escape Sequences

| Sequence | Effect |
|---|---|
| `\n` | Newline |
| `\t` | Tab |
| `\"` | Double quote inside string |
| `\\` | Literal backslash |
| `\0` | Null character (end of string) |

