## 10. Functions

Functions let you break your program into **reusable, named pieces of logic**.

### Anatomy

```c
return_type function_name(parameter_type parameter_name)
{
    // body
    return value;  // omit if return_type is void
}
```

### Full Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Prototype — tells the compiler the function exists before main()
int square(int n);

int main(void)
{
    printf("Square of 5 = %i\n", square(5));
}

// Definition
int square(int n)
{
    return n * n;
}
```

### void Functions

```c
void greet(string name)
{
    printf("Hello, %s!\n", name);
    // no return statement needed
}
```

### Variable Scope

```c
int global = 10;   // global — accessible everywhere (use sparingly)

int main(void)
{
    int local = 5;   // local — only accessible inside main()
}
```

> **Best practice:** Avoid global variables. Pass values via parameters and return values instead.

